        "notes": "Pass 1 (author sourcing, CM-41 S2, 2026-10-30): the National Institute of Biological Resources species page for 반달가슴곰 (its description, read through the page's content service) and the National Institute of Korean Language Standard Korean Dictionary entry. Both give the white half-moon mark on the chest and the winter sleep in a den or a hollow tree; NIBR says it likes acorns in particular and honey in particular. The endangered status and the 2004 Jirisan project are the Stage 4 Nature note (n1 there) and are not repeated. English paraphrase.\nPass 2 (culture and facts check, 2026-10-30): NIBR (앞가슴에 반달 모양의 흰색 무늬; 특히 도토리를 좋아하나; 꿀을 특히 좋아하며; 겨울에 굴속 또는 나무 구멍 ... 동면) and NIKL (앞가슴에 반달 모양의 흰무늬; 나무의 빈 구멍이나 굴에 들어가 겨울잠) match each clause. Institutions named as on the access date. No designation number. Holds.\nPass 3 (language and learning design, 2026-10-30): the token 반달가슴곰 with 은 after its final ㅁ; es oso negro asiático, as the scene's es names it. Reviser: the ko 잠을 자요 became 겨울잠을 자요, the word the NIKL entry uses. The Stage 4 Nature note (endangered, the 2004 Jirisan project) is not repeated here.\nPass 4 (third pass, 2026-10-30): the revised en, es and ko read against both sources and F-CURR-002 §5.1 and §7.7 again; each claim says no more than its quotes; no copy claims native-speaker or expert review. The independent MAR culture review of the PR re-reads the sources."
